msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:6
msgid "Performance"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:8
msgid "The **Performance** application runs the conversation a company has with its people about how work is going: what was agreed, what happened, what it was worth and what comes next. It covers the whole cycle — objectives and their check-ins, a structured questionnaire, a self-assessment and a manager assessment, an optional multi-rater round, a rating that is calibrated before it is released, the employee's own written reply, a development plan that outlives the review, and a retention period after which the review is proposed for deletion."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:15
msgid "It is built for a small or mid-sized company: one HR officer, line managers who conduct two or three reviews a year each, and employees who should be able to read their own review without asking anybody's permission — and should never be able to read anybody else's."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:20
msgid "A performance review is one of the most sensitive records a company keeps about a person. The confidentiality architecture is not an option you switch on: it is how the data is laid out. Read :doc:`performance/confidentiality` before you launch a real cycle, and before you grant anybody the :guilabel:`Officer` access level."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:26
msgid "The five modules"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:28
msgid "The application is a family of five modules. Installing **Performance Reviews** installs the first two; the other three are optional and can be added at any time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:32
msgid "Module"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:32
msgid "What it adds"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:34
msgid "**Performance Reviews** (``hr_review``)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:34
msgid "The application itself: rating scales, questionnaires, review cycles, the review packet, the confidential assessment, sign-off, reminders and retention."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:38
msgid "**Objectives** (``hr_objective``)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:38
msgid "Objectives, key results and development goals with a real measurement and dated check-ins. Installed with the application, and usable on its own."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:42
msgid "**Multi-rater Reviews (360)** (``hr_review_360``)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:42
msgid "Peer, direct report, skip-level and outside raters, nomination and approval, the anonymity threshold and the portal form outside raters answer on."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:46
msgid "**Review Competencies** (``hr_review_competency``)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:46
msgid "A competency framework over Odoo's skills: behavioural anchors, a per-job requirement matrix, gap analysis and a non-destructive write-back."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:50
msgid "**Hungary - Performance Reviews** (``l10n_hu_hr_review``)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:50
msgid "Trial-period (*próbaidő*) reviews counted from the contract, the probation decision record and its printed Hungarian document, and Hungarian master data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:56
msgid "Technical names, for whoever installs them: ``hr_review`` (the application, listed as *Performance Reviews*), ``hr_objective``, ``hr_review_360``, ``hr_review_competency`` and ``l10n_hu_hr_review``. The application appears in the apps switcher as **Performance**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:61
msgid "Who does what"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:63
msgid "Three access levels sit in the :guilabel:`Performance` category of a user's access rights, each one implying the one above it. Being in a group says *what a person may do*; the record rules decide *whose records they may do it to*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:68
msgid "Access level"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:68
msgid "Who it is for"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:68
msgid "What it allows"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:70
msgid "(none)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:70
msgid "Every employee"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:70
msgid "Read their own review, answer their own questions, acknowledge or comment on a review that has been shared with them, and run their own objectives."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:75
msgid ":guilabel:`Reviewer`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:75
msgid "Line managers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:75
msgid "Everything above, plus conducting the reviews of the people who report to them: the manager assessment, the rating, the notes, the panel and the development plan of *their own* reviews only."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:81
msgid ":guilabel:`Officer`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:81
#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/conducting_a_review.rst:38
msgid "HR"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:81
msgid "Runs and sees every review in the companies they are allowed to work in: cycles, launches, reassignment, legal hold, unlocking and the purge."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:86
msgid ":guilabel:`Administrator`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:86
msgid "Whoever sets the system up"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:86
msgid "Everything above, plus the configuration: rating scales, questionnaires, reminder templates and retention."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:92
msgid ":guilabel:`Reviewer` is not a job title, it is an access level. A line manager who has it still only reaches the reviews of the people whose reviews name them as reviewer. Granting :guilabel:`Officer` to a line manager gives them every review in the company, including their own manager's — that is almost never what is wanted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:98
msgid "Setting it up the first time"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:100
msgid "The order matters: a cycle cannot be launched on a questionnaire that has not been released, and a questionnaire cannot be released without a rating scale."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:103
msgid "**Install the application.** Go to :menuselection:`Apps`, search for *Performance Reviews* and install it. Add **Multi-rater Reviews (360)**, **Review Competencies** and, in Hungary, **Hungary - Performance Reviews** if you need them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:106
msgid "**Grant access rights.** In :menuselection:`Settings --> Users & Companies --> Users`, give yourself :guilabel:`Administrator` in the :guilabel:`Performance` category, give HR :guilabel:`Officer`, and give the line managers :guilabel:`Reviewer`. Everybody else needs nothing: a plain internal user already reaches their own review and their own objectives."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:110
msgid "**Create a rating scale.** No scale is shipped on purpose — see :doc:`performance/configuration`. Either write your own at :menuselection:`Performance --> Configuration --> Rating Scales`, or let :menuselection:`Performance --> Configuration --> Starter Rating Scale` write a five-level one for you to rename and reword. The Hungarian pack ships a Hungarian scale with behavioural descriptions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:116
msgid "**Build a questionnaire** under :menuselection:`Performance --> Configuration --> Questionnaires`, then click :guilabel:`Release`. A released questionnaire is frozen; that is what keeps the answers stored under it meaningful."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:119
msgid "**Set the company options.** In :menuselection:`Settings --> Employees`, in the :guilabel:`Performance Reviews` block, set the default questionnaire and scale, the retention period, and — if you want them — the automatic review intervals."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:122
msgid "**Check that the employees have a manager.** A review needs somebody to conduct it. An employee with no manager on their employee record is reported and skipped when a cycle is launched, never silently included."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:125
msgid "**Launch a first cycle** from :menuselection:`Performance --> Cycles --> Review Cycles`. The launch shows you exactly who is in scope and who is being skipped, before anything is created."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:130
msgid "Run the first cycle on one department. The launch preview, the reminder schedule and the sign-off step are all easier to judge on twenty reviews than on two hundred."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:134
msgid "What the application deliberately refuses to do"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:136
msgid "Several of these will look like missing features until you need them. They are decisions, and each one is explained where it applies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:139
msgid "**A rating scale that is already in use cannot be re-valued.** Changing what \"3\" is worth would silently rewrite every rating ever given on it. Create a new version instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:141
msgid "**A released questionnaire cannot be restructured.** Answers were given under its wording."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:142
msgid "**A multi-rater round that cannot keep its anonymity promise is refused at launch**, not at release time — by release time people have already answered under the promise. See :doc:`performance/multi_rater`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:145
msgid "**Retention is proposed, never executed automatically.** There is no cron that deletes reviews. A person runs a dry run, reads what it lists, and confirms. See :doc:`performance/confidentiality`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:148
msgid "**A review under legal hold is never purged and never deleted**, whatever its retention date says."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:150
msgid "**A trial-period termination records the fact, not a justification.** See :doc:`performance/probation_hu`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:152
msgid "**The employee's disagreement is recorded in their own words.** Sign-off is not a checkbox that says the employee agrees; it is a record that they were shown the review, with room for what they think of it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:170
msgid ":doc:`employees` — employee records, managers and departments, which every review reads"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance.rst:171
msgid ":doc:`../general/users/access_rights` — how Odoo groups and record rules work"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:3
msgid "Competencies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:5
msgid "The **Review Competencies** module puts a competency framework over Odoo's skills. It answers three questions a rating on its own cannot: *what does this job need*, *what does level 3 actually mean here*, and *where is the difference*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:9
msgid "It adds a :guilabel:`Competencies` page to the review, and two configuration lists under :menuselection:`Performance --> Configuration`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:15
msgid "Behavioural anchors"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:17
msgid "A rater who reads *Expert (90%)* learns nothing. A rater who reads what somebody at that level actually does in this job rates the same way as their colleague two aisles down."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:28
msgid "Go to :menuselection:`Performance --> Configuration --> Behavioural Anchors` and write one description per skill level:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:31
msgid ":guilabel:`Skill` and :guilabel:`Level` — what is being described."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:32
msgid ":guilabel:`Job Position` — the job this description was written for. Leave it empty for the description that applies to every job."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:34
msgid ":guilabel:`Description` — what somebody at this level does in practice. This is the text the rater reads while assessing the competency."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:38
msgid "Write anchors in observable behaviour, not adjectives. \"Loads and unloads without a spotter, spots a damaged pallet before it moves, and trains a newcomer on the aisle rules\" tells a rater what to look for. \"Strong operational skills\" does not."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:42
msgid "A job-specific description takes precedence over the general one, which is why the same skill can mean different things for a warehouse operator and for a buyer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:48
msgid "The per-job requirement matrix"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:50
msgid ":menuselection:`Performance --> Configuration --> Job Competencies` states what each job needs: a :guilabel:`Job Position`, a skill, the :guilabel:`Required Level` it needs, and a :guilabel:`Weight` saying how much that competency counts against the others."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:54
msgid "A requirement written down once is a requirement the review can measure against. Without it, every review argues about the expectation before it can discuss the person. A job requires a given skill at exactly one level; two contradictory rows are refused."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:61
msgid "Competencies in a review"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:63
msgid "For a questionnaire to assess competencies, give it a section whose :guilabel:`Kind` is *Competencies* — see :doc:`configuration`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:74
msgid "When the review is opened, one line is created for each of the employee's current skills **plus** each skill their job requires that they do not have yet. The second half matters: a competency the person is missing entirely is exactly the one worth discussing, and it would be invisible if the lines came only from what they already hold."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:79
msgid "Each line carries:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:82
msgid "Column"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:82
#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/review_cycles.rst:18
msgid "What it is"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:84
msgid ":guilabel:`Required Level`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:84
msgid "Copied from the job's requirement matrix when the review opened. Empty when the job asks nothing of this competency."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:88
msgid ":guilabel:`Previous Level`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:88
msgid "The level the employee held when the review opened. A snapshot; it is never recomputed afterwards."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:92
msgid ":guilabel:`Employee's View`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:92
msgid "What the employee thinks their level is."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:94
msgid ":guilabel:`Manager's View`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:94
msgid "What the reviewer thinks it is."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:96
msgid ":guilabel:`Agreed Level`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:96
msgid "What the two of them settled on. This is the **only** level ever written back onto the employee."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:100
msgid ":guilabel:`Gap`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:100
msgid "The agreed level minus the required level, in percentage points of level progress. Negative means the job asks for more than what was agreed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:104
msgid ":guilabel:`Certification`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:104
msgid "Tick when the level rests on a certificate rather than on an opinion."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:107
msgid ":guilabel:`Valid From` / :guilabel:`Valid To`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:107
msgid "When the agreed level holds from and until. For a certificate, its expiry date."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:110
msgid ":guilabel:`Note`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:110
msgid "What was said about this competency, for the record and for the next review."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:114
msgid "The gap is shown with a colour on every list that displays it, and the standalone list totals it. A search filter, :guilabel:`Below the Requirement`, brings up every line where the agreed level falls short of what the job asks for."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:119
msgid "A level that is not set counts as zero when the gap is computed. A person assessed against a requirement they have never been assessed on shows the full gap, which is the honest answer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:123
msgid "The :guilabel:`Competencies` page is visible from the :guilabel:`Reviewer` access level up. The employee does not read the competency lines on the review; what reaches them is the agreed level, once it has been written onto their own skills at closing. Discuss the assessment in the review conversation rather than expecting them to find it on the form."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:131
msgid "Writing agreed levels back to the employee"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:133
msgid "When the review is closed, the levels agreed on this page are written back onto the employee's skills. Three properties of that write-back are worth knowing:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:136
msgid "**It is a difference, not a replacement.** Only the competencies whose agreed level actually differs from what the employee currently holds are touched, and the existing skill row is updated in place. Nothing is deleted and recreated, so the employee's skill history and anything else pointing at those rows survive."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:140
msgid "**A skill the employee did not have yet is added**, at the agreed level."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:141
msgid "**A competency whose skill type has been archived is left alone and reported.** An archived skill type is out of the framework; rewriting a level on it would resurrect a classification somebody deliberately retired."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:145
msgid "Only the :guilabel:`Agreed Level` is ever written back. The employee's own view and the manager's view stay on the review as a record of the conversation, and neither one silently becomes the truth."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:150
msgid "Every change the write-back makes is logged on the **confidential assessment** of the review, never on the review itself, together with the old and new level of each skill and the name of the person who closed the review. A level a manager wrote down is a rating, and ratings do not go in a log the employee reads. See :doc:`confidentiality`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:158
#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/reporting.rst:145
msgid "Competency evolution"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:160
msgid ":menuselection:`Performance --> Reporting --> Competency Evolution` opens a pivot of the gap across skill types, skills and cycles, with a bar chart alongside."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:163
msgid "Once a few cycles have closed, that view stops being a report and becomes a training plan: a skill with a persistent negative gap across a department is a course to book, not an opinion to argue about."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:167
msgid "Access to it follows the reviews it is built from: a reviewer sees the competency lines of the people they review, and an officer sees them all. Employees have no access to this view."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:171
msgid ":doc:`configuration` — the *Competencies* section kind on a questionnaire"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/competencies.rst:172
msgid ":doc:`conducting_a_review` — where the write-back sits in the closing sequence"
msgstr ""

