msgid "The rating, the calibration decision and the scores"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:31
msgid "The released rating only"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:31
msgid "Their own reviews"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:34
msgid "The reviewer's private working notes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:34
msgid "Their own only"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:34
msgid "Break-glass, logged"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:37
msgid "Who was asked for feedback (the panel)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:37
msgid "Named raters only"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:42
msgid "Their own written comment at sign-off"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:42
msgid "Read and write, once"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:46
msgid "Two of those rows deserve more than a table cell."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:49
msgid "The confidential assessment"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:51
msgid "The rating, the reviewer's proposal, the calibration decision, the composite scores, the potential and flight-risk entries and the succession note do **not** live on the review. They live on a separate record, reached from the :guilabel:`Rating` page of the review form, which carries its own access rules — and none of those rules ever matches the employee under review."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:56
msgid "That distinction matters more than it looks. If the rating were a hidden field on the review, its confidentiality would rest on a screen setting, and an export, a filter, a grouping or a report could still reach it. Because it is a separate record, an employee looking at their own review does not have the field at all: not in the interface, not in an export, not in a group-by."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:63
msgid "The employee sees a rating exactly once: the **released** rating, on the review that has been shared with them, and only after somebody decided to share it. The rating the reviewer originally proposed, and the reason it was changed, are never shown to them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:68
msgid "The original proposal and the final rating are both kept, side by side, permanently. A calibration that moves a rating away from the proposal is refused unless the person who moves it writes down why. That is an audit trail, not a formality: it is what lets a rating be explained a year later."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:74
msgid "What is written in the log of a review"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:76
msgid "The message log — the chatter — of a review is treated as a document the employee reads, because they can. Every confidential entry (a rating being set, a calibration, the decision to release, an unlock and its reason, a competency written back to the employee's skills) is logged on the **confidential assessment** instead, where the employee has no access."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:81
msgid "The same reasoning applies to to-dos. A scheduled activity is readable by anybody who can read the document it hangs on, so a review activity is never placed on the review: each person who owes something is chased on their own participation record. An employee therefore cannot work out who else was asked for feedback by looking at the to-do list of their own review."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:89
msgid "What the employee can always see"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:91
msgid "Whatever else is configured, an employee reaches, for their own review and without asking:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:93
msgid "the review record itself — the period, the questionnaire, the due date, the status and who their reviewer is;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:95
msgid "every question they were asked, and every answer they gave;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:96
msgid "once the review has been shared with them: the reviewer's answers to the questions marked :guilabel:`Shared With Employee` on the questionnaire, and the released rating;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:98
msgid "the development plan agreed with them, including the actions carried over from the previous review and what came of them;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:100
msgid "the printed review packet — the same document, produced from the same template, whoever prints it;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:102
msgid "their own comment at sign-off, in their own words."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:104
msgid "And, deliberately, nothing else: not the panel, not another employee's review, not the reviewer's working notes, not the calibration, and not an individual answer from an anonymous round."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:111
msgid "Answering a subject access request"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:113
msgid "There is no button labelled *subject access request*, and none is needed: the employee already holds the readable part of their own file, and the confidential part is a short, well-defined list an officer can produce by hand."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:117
msgid "Ask the employee to print their own reviews from :menuselection:`Performance --> Reviews --> My Reviews`, using :guilabel:`Print`. That is the review packet: the questionnaire with the answers, the released rating, the development plan and the sign-off, with the retention date stated in the footer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:121
msgid "As an officer, add what the packet does not carry, if the request asks for it: the rating the reviewer proposed and the calibration reason, from the confidential assessment; the scores; and the potential, flight-risk and succession entries if they were used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:124
msgid "Add the reviewer's working notes about that employee, if any exist."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:127
msgid "A reviewer's working note is **not** secret from the employee it is about. It is not routinely shared, which is a different thing. Anybody writing notes should write them knowing that a subject access request, a labour dispute or a court can bring them out. The application does not pretend otherwise, and an HR policy should not either."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:132
msgid "An officer who has to read another reviewer's notes does so through a break-glass action, which records on the confidential log of the review that they did. Use it when you have a reason, and expect the fact to be visible afterwards."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:139
msgid "Retention and the purge"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:141
msgid "When a review is closed it is stamped with a retention date: the day it closed plus the retention period of its cycle, or of the company when the cycle does not state one. The default is 36 months, the Hungarian limitation period for labour claims; set it to whatever your own legal advice says."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:146
msgid "The retention date is a **proposal**. Nothing happens on that day. No scheduled job deletes anything, ever."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:150
msgid "Running a purge"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:152
msgid "Look at what is due first, at :menuselection:`Performance --> Configuration --> Reviews Past their Retention Date`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:154
msgid "Open :menuselection:`Performance --> Configuration --> Retention and Purge`, choose the :guilabel:`Companies` and the :guilabel:`Retention Reached On` date, and click :guilabel:`Dry Run`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:157
msgid "Read what the dry run reports on the :guilabel:`Dry Run` page: how many reviews would be anonymised per company, how many are refused because they are under legal hold, and the oldest and newest retention dates concerned. The :guilabel:`Reviews Concerned` and :guilabel:`Refused, Under Legal Hold` pages name them. Nothing has been touched at this point."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:162
msgid "If you are satisfied, click :guilabel:`Anonymise the Listed Reviews` and confirm."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:173
msgid "A purge **anonymises**; it does not delete. Each review keeps its row, its dates, the rating that was released and the sign-off, and is archived. What is removed is the free text and the identity of the raters: the employee's own comment, the text and the comment of every answer, the body of every working note, and the identity of every rater other than the employee themselves. With the multi-rater pack installed, the outside raters' names and addresses, who nominated and who approved each rater, and the fingerprint of every feedback link go with them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:181
msgid "This is not reversible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:183
msgid "A single run touches at most 500 reviews. If more are due, the result says so and you run it again."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:187
msgid "A review is only anonymised while it is genuinely past its retention date. If a review was reopened, or its retention date changed, between the dry run and the execution, it is refused and named in the result rather than being purged on stale information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:192
msgid "Legal hold"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:194
msgid "An officer can tick :guilabel:`Legal Hold` in the :guilabel:`Retention` group of the review form. A review under legal hold:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:197
msgid "is refused by the purge, whatever its retention date says, and is named in the result so that you can see it was refused rather than missed;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:199
msgid "cannot be deleted at all."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:201
msgid "Use it the moment a dispute, an inspection or a claim becomes foreseeable, and take it off when the matter is closed. It is the one switch that overrides the retention schedule."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:207
msgid "What this does not protect you from"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:209
msgid "Being clear about the boundary is part of being trustworthy about what is inside it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:211
msgid "**An officer sees everything in their companies.** That is what the access level means. Grant it to as few people as the work allows, and review the list of holders periodically."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:213
msgid "**A database administrator sees everything.** No application rule constrains somebody with server or database access."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:215
msgid "**A printed packet leaves the system.** Once a review has been printed or exported, retention and legal hold no longer reach that copy."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:217
msgid "**Anonymity is a threshold, not a cryptographic guarantee.** It stops a small group being identified from counts and averages. It does not stop somebody being recognised by their writing style in a pooled comment — see :doc:`multi_rater`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:220
msgid "**Notes are private, not privileged.** See the warning above."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:223
msgid ":doc:`multi_rater` — how the anonymity threshold works and what it refuses to do"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:224
msgid ":doc:`conducting_a_review` — sign-off, unlocking, and what each one records"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/confidentiality.rst:225
msgid ":doc:`../../general/users/access_rights` — the Odoo access rights every rule above sits on"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:5
msgid "Everything on this page lives under :menuselection:`Performance --> Configuration` and needs the :guilabel:`Administrator` access level of the :guilabel:`Performance` category. Set the rating scale up first, then the questionnaire that uses it, then the company options."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:12
msgid "Rating scales"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:14
msgid "A rating scale is a list of levels. Each level carries a name, a numeric value and a description of the behaviour it stands for."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:18
msgid "Why a level carries a number"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:20
msgid "Without a number, a rating is a word. *Good* cannot be averaged, compared with last year, plotted against a department, or weighted against another question. The number is what makes a rating comparable; the description is what makes it fair. A scale that has one without the other produces either arithmetic nobody trusts or opinions nobody can aggregate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:26
msgid "If you would rather not show the numbers, tick :guilabel:`Hide Numeric Value` on the scale. The levels are then shown by name only, while everything that has to compute still can."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:30
msgid "Creating a scale"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:32
msgid "Go to :menuselection:`Performance --> Configuration --> Rating Scales` and create a record."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:33
msgid "Give it a :guilabel:`Name` and, if you like, a short :guilabel:`Code`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:34
msgid "Leave :guilabel:`Company` empty to make the scale a shared catalogue entry every company reads, or set it to restrict the scale to one company."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:36
msgid "Decide on :guilabel:`Allow Not Applicable`. When it is on, a rater may state that they are not in a position to judge a question instead of guessing. When it is off, a question that uses the scale has to be answered."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:39
msgid "On the :guilabel:`Levels` page, add one line per level:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:42
#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/review_cycles.rst:169
msgid "Field"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:42
msgid "What it is for"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:44
msgid ":guilabel:`Name`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:44
msgid "What the rater picks, for example *Meets expectations*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:46
msgid ":guilabel:`Value`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:46
msgid "The numeric anchor. This is the reason the level exists as a record: it is what makes two ratings comparable and what every average is computed from."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:50
msgid ":guilabel:`Description`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:50
msgid "The behaviour a rater should recognise before picking this level. It is shown at rating time, and it is the single most effective thing you can write to make two managers rate the same way."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:55
msgid ":guilabel:`Below Expectation`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:55
msgid "Marks the level as under the bar. Used by the analysis views and by decorations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:58
msgid ":guilabel:`Count In Average`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:58
msgid "On by default. Turn it off for a level that should not drag a score, for instance a *Not observed* level."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:61
msgid ":guilabel:`Target Share (%)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:61
msgid "Advisory distribution guidance for calibration conversations. It is never enforced: nothing stops a department from rating everybody at the top level."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:67
msgid "Write the :guilabel:`Description` of every level. It costs an afternoon, and it is what stops *3* meaning \"fine, I suppose\" for one manager and \"genuinely strong\" for another."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:79
msgid "Starting from a scale somebody else wrote"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:81
msgid "If you would rather not start from an empty form, open :menuselection:`Performance --> Configuration --> Starter Rating Scale`. Give the scale a :guilabel:`Scale Name`, decide on :guilabel:`Allow Not Applicable`, and click :guilabel:`Create the Scale`. It writes an ordinary five-level scale — from *Well below expectations* to *Outstanding* — that you then rename and reword. It is a starting point, not a recommendation, and the wizard says so."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:88
msgid "If you already have a scale, the wizard warns you that this creates another one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:91
msgid "Nothing is shipped, on purpose"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:93
msgid "No rating scale and no questionnaire come with the application. Generic seed content would encode one company's culture and one language's wording, and every customer would then spend their first week editing it out. The Hungarian pack does ship a five-level Hungarian scale with behavioural descriptions and four Hungarian questionnaires, because those are written for one specific practice — see :doc:`probation_hu`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:100
msgid "A scale that has been used is frozen"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:102
msgid "As soon as a rating has been recorded on a scale, its levels can no longer be added, removed or re-valued, and the form says so in a banner. Re-valuing a level would silently rewrite the meaning of every rating ever given on it: last year's *3* would quietly become worth something else, and every average, comparison and trend computed since would be wrong."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:107
msgid "To change a frozen scale, click :guilabel:`New Version`. That copies the scale into a new, editable one, points the old scale at its successor and archives it. Existing reviews keep the version they were rated on; new cycles pick up the new one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:112
msgid "A used scale cannot be deleted either. Archive it instead: the reviews rated on it stay readable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:118
msgid "Questionnaires"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:120
msgid "A questionnaire is what people actually fill in. It is made of **sections**, and a section is made of **questions**. Go to :menuselection:`Performance --> Configuration --> Questionnaires`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:132
msgid "Sections"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:134
msgid "Each section has a :guilabel:`Name`, a :guilabel:`Sequence` and a :guilabel:`Kind`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:136
msgid "**Questions** — the ordinary case: the questions you write under it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:137
msgid "**Objectives of the period** — no questions of its own. It renders the objectives that were in scope for the period, so the conversation starts from what was agreed. See :doc:`objectives`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:140
msgid "**Competencies** — filled in by the **Review Competencies** module. See :doc:`competencies`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:141
msgid "**Free text** — a section with no structure, for a narrative."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:144
msgid "Audiences"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:146
msgid "Both a section and a question state who is asked to answer it:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:149
msgid "Audience"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:149
msgid "Who answers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:151
#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:134
msgid "Everybody"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:151
msgid "The employee, the reviewer and every other rater."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:153
msgid "The self-assessment only."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:155
msgid "The manager"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:155
msgid "The reviewer only."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:157
msgid "Other raters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:157
msgid "Peers, direct reports, skip levels and outside contacts only."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:159
msgid "Same as the section"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:159
msgid "On a question: inherit whatever the section says. This is the default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:163
msgid "This is what lets one questionnaire serve the whole review. The employee is asked the questions addressed to them, the reviewer is asked theirs, and a multi-rater round asks the *Other raters* ones — out of a single stored set of answers, which is why aggregation, weighting and the anonymity threshold behave consistently everywhere."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:169
msgid "Questions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:171
msgid "A question carries:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:173
msgid ":guilabel:`Name` — the question itself, and :guilabel:`Guidance` under it for what the person answering should think about."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:175
msgid ":guilabel:`Answer Type`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:178
msgid "Answer type"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:178
msgid "What the person answering sees"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:180
msgid "Rating"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:180
msgid "The levels of the rating scale, with their descriptions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:182
msgid "Free text"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:182
msgid "A text box."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:184
#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:84
msgid "Number"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:184
msgid "A numeric field."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:186
msgid "Yes / No"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:186
msgid "A checkbox."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:188
msgid "Multiple choice"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:188
msgid "The options you defined on the question, each carrying its own numeric value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:192
msgid ":guilabel:`Rating Scale` — set it only to override the questionnaire's default scale for this one question."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:194
msgid ":guilabel:`Required` — a required question blocks submission until it is answered."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:195
msgid ":guilabel:`Comment Policy` — *No comment*, *Comment optional* or *Comment required*. Use *Comment required* on the summary rating: a number with no sentence behind it is the hardest thing to defend in a review conversation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:198
msgid ":guilabel:`Weight` — only used when :guilabel:`Use Weights` is on for the questionnaire. Leave weights off unless you have a reason; while they are off, a score is a plain mean of the rating answers whose level counts in the average."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:205
msgid "The \"shared with the employee\" decision"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:207
msgid "Every question carries :guilabel:`Shared With Employee`. It decides whether the answer to that question is shown to the employee when the review is released to them. It is on by default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:210
msgid "Turn it off deliberately and rarely. A question the employee never sees is a question they cannot answer back to, and a review made mostly of such questions is not a review, it is a file. The legitimate cases are narrow: a succession or readiness judgement that is an input to a management decision rather than feedback to a person."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:216
msgid ":guilabel:`Shared With Employee` decides what is shown **when the review is shared**, not whether an answer is confidential in general. HR reads every answer. The rating and the calibration are confidential for a different reason and by a different mechanism — see :doc:`confidentiality`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:222
msgid "Releasing and versioning"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:224
msgid "A questionnaire is :guilabel:`Draft` while you build it. Click :guilabel:`Release` to make it usable by a cycle. Release refuses a questionnaire that is not finished, and says which part:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:227
msgid "a questionnaire with no section;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:228
msgid "a *Questions* section with no question;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:229
msgid "a multiple choice question with fewer than two options;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:230
msgid "a rating question with no rating scale."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:232
msgid "Once released, the structure is frozen: sections and questions can no longer be added, removed or restructured, and the form says so in a banner. Answers were given under that wording, and changing a question after the fact would leave an answer attached to a question nobody was asked."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/configuration.rst:237
