#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:105
msgid "Alignment, and why it is not roll-up"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:107
msgid ":guilabel:`Aligned To` points an objective at another one. That is a statement about *meaning*: this objective exists in order to serve that one. It builds the alignment tree, it lets you group and report along it, and it costs nothing — the parent's progress is unaffected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:111
msgid ":guilabel:`Roll Up Children` is a statement about *arithmetic*: when it is ticked, the objective stops computing its own measurement and takes the weighted average of the objectives aligned under it instead, using each child's :guilabel:`Weight`. An objective that rolls up with no children sits at 0%."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:117
msgid "Align freely; roll up sparingly. Alignment describes how the work fits together, which is almost always worth recording. Roll-up hands your number to somebody else's data quality: a parent that rolls up is only as honest as the children under it, and a child nobody checks in on drags a whole department's figure down without anybody noticing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:122
msgid "An objective cannot be aligned to one of the objectives aligned under it; a cycle is refused."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:127
msgid "Who can see an objective"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:129
msgid ":guilabel:`Visibility` decides who reaches an objective:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:132
msgid "Visibility"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:132
msgid "Who reaches the objective"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:134
msgid "Every colleague in the company may read it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:136
msgid "Employee and Management Line"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:136
msgid "The employees it is assigned to, their line managers and its follow-up owners. This is the default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:139
msgid "Employee Only"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:139
msgid "The employees it is assigned to. Neither the line manager nor the follow-up owners reach it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:143
msgid "HR officers reach every objective whatever this says."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:145
msgid "*Employee Only* is the setting to use for a development goal about a personal difficulty. Note that it excludes the line manager, which is usually the point, and that HR still reaches it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:151
msgid "Check-ins"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:153
msgid "A check-in states where an objective stands on a given date, how confident its owner is, and why. It is what turns a percentage into something the next review can actually talk about."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:164
msgid "Open the objective and click :guilabel:`Check In`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:165
msgid "Enter the :guilabel:`Date`, the :guilabel:`New Value` the objective reached, and your :guilabel:`Confidence`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:167
msgid "Under :guilabel:`What happened`, write what moved, what is in the way, and what happens next."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:169
msgid "Recording a check-in writes the new value and the confidence back onto the objective, stores a snapshot of the resulting progress on the check-in itself, and logs the change. The history is on the :guilabel:`Check-ins` page of the objective and behind its :guilabel:`Check-ins` smart button."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:175
msgid "Keeping the rhythm"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:177
msgid ":guilabel:`Checkin Frequency` (*No Reminder*, *Weekly*, *Every Two Weeks*, *Monthly*) sets :guilabel:`Next Checkin Date` from the last check-in, or from the start date when there has not been one yet."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:181
msgid "There is no nagging job behind this. \"Stale\" is simply a filter: the :guilabel:`Needs a Check-in` filter in the search view lists every objective whose next check-in date has arrived. Combine it with :guilabel:`My Team` to prepare a one-to-one, or with :guilabel:`Late` — deadline passed, not achieved and not cancelled — before a cycle launch."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:187
msgid "The reason this matters at review time: an objective with monthly check-ins produces twelve dated facts a year. An objective with none produces one argument in December."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:193
msgid "Templates and bulk assignment"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:195
msgid "When the same objective goes to many people, write it once as a template."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:197
msgid "Go to :menuselection:`Employees --> Objectives --> Templates` and create a record, or tick :guilabel:`Is Template` on an existing one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:199
msgid "A template carries **no** employees — that is enforced. It is the shared wording, not somebody's objective."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:201
msgid "Build the tree under it if you want key results to come with it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:202
msgid "Use :guilabel:`Instantiate Objective Template`, choose the :guilabel:`Employees`, and optionally override :guilabel:`Start Date` and :guilabel:`Deadline` (leave them empty to keep the dates on the template)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:205
msgid ":guilabel:`Include Aligned Objectives` also copies the objectives aligned under the template and aligns each copy under the copy of the template."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:207
msgid ":guilabel:`Keep Link to Template` records on every new objective which template it came from, so the instances of one template can be reported on together."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:210
msgid "Each selected employee gets **their own copy**. They can then be measured, checked in on and closed independently, which is what you want: one shared record with fifteen owners cannot say that eleven of them are on track."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:215
msgid "A template is explicit, never inferred. An ordinary objective that happens to have lost its employees does not quietly become a template."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:221
msgid "Who can do what"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:223
msgid "An employee creates and edits the objectives assigned to them, and records their own check-ins."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:225
msgid "A line manager, and anybody named as a follow-up owner, does the same for the objectives of the people they manage — except for the ones marked *Employee Only*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:227
msgid "Nobody but HR can delete an objective. Set its status to *Cancelled* instead; the record of what was agreed and then dropped is usually worth more than a clean list."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:229
msgid "HR officers reach everything."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:232
msgid ":doc:`conducting_a_review` — how the period's objectives are pulled into a review"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/objectives.rst:233
msgid ":doc:`configuration` — the *Objectives of the period* section kind"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/probation_hu.rst:3
msgid "Trial-period reviews in Hungary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/probation_hu.rst:5
msgid "The **Hungary - Performance Reviews** module adds what Hungarian practice needs on top of the generic application: trial-period (*próbaidő*) reviews counted from the employment contract, a probation decision record with its own printed Hungarian document, and Hungarian master data to start from."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/probation_hu.rst:10
msgid "It contains data and wiring only. Nothing here changes how a review works elsewhere."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/probation_hu.rst:15
msgid "How the plan counts from the contract"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/probation_hu.rst:17
msgid "A trial-period review does not count months from an anniversary. It reads the employee's **running employment contract** and works backwards from the end of the trial period, so that the decision is prepared while there is still time to take it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/probation_hu.rst:21
msgid "The **anchor date** of the plan is the contract's start date. An employee with no running Hungarian contract falls back to the generic anchor."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/probation_hu.rst:23
msgid "The **decision review** falls due :guilabel:`Probation Decision Lead Time (days)` before the trial period ends — 10 days by default. Set it in :menuselection:`Settings --> Employees`, in the :guilabel:`Performance Reviews` block. It has to leave enough time to hold the conversation, write the decision and hand it over **while the trial period is still running**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/probation_hu.rst:27
msgid "Optionally, :guilabel:`Probation Checkpoints` also plans a short review 30 and 60 days after the employment started, in front of the decision review. It is off by default: whether every probationer is reviewed twice on the way is your decision, not the module's."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/probation_hu.rst:31
msgid "A checkpoint is only planned when it falls before the decision review, so a short trial period does not produce a checkpoint after the decision it was meant to inform. An employee whose contract has no trial period end date has no probation plan at all."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/probation_hu.rst:36
msgid "A trial period shorter than the lead time still gets its decision review; it simply falls due on the first day of the employment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/probation_hu.rst:40
msgid "Running a probation cycle"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/probation_hu.rst:42
msgid "Create a cycle of the type *Probation*, on the *Próbaidős értékelés* questionnaire, with a population filter that selects the people on trial."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/probation_hu.rst:44
msgid "Launch it. Launching a probation cycle creates **no** review — it puts the cycle into :guilabel:`Running`, and from then on a daily job creates each review as it falls due."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/probation_hu.rst:46
msgid "Make sure :guilabel:`Plan Reviews Automatically` is on in the company settings. It is the master switch of that job: while it is off, a probation cycle creates nothing at all."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/probation_hu.rst:49
msgid "At most one review per employee is created per run, and none while a previous probation review of the same cycle is still open — a checkpoint nobody has answered yet must not be buried under the next one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/probation_hu.rst:54
msgid "Probationers who have no manager on their employee record cannot be reviewed. The cycle's log records **how many** were skipped for that reason but never their names, since the log of a cycle is read by more people than the list itself should be."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/probation_hu.rst:61
msgid "The probation decision"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/probation_hu.rst:63
msgid "The decision is a record of its own, not a field on the review. It has its own document, its own lock and its own audit trail, because it is the thing that gets handed to a person and may have to be produced years later."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/probation_hu.rst:75
msgid "Reach it from the :guilabel:`Probation Decision` button, or the page of the same name, on a probation review — both visible to an officer — or from :menuselection:`Performance --> Reviews --> Probation Decisions`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/probation_hu.rst:79
msgid "Fill in:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/probation_hu.rst:81
msgid ":guilabel:`Employee` and :guilabel:`Contract`, which bring in :guilabel:`Employment Start` and :guilabel:`End of Trial Period`;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/probation_hu.rst:83
msgid ":guilabel:`Decision Date` — the date the decision was taken. It cannot be earlier than the start of the employment it decides about;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/hr/performance/probation_hu.rst:85
msgid ":guilabel:`Decided By` — the person who *took* the decision, not the person who typed it in;"
msgstr ""

